WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:10.970
In this video, you turn on the monitoring stack, let it collect metrics for a while, and then tour what it gives you: metrics in the UI, dashboards in Grafana, and queries in Prometheus.

00:00:10.970 --> 00:00:15.830
Under Advanced, Add-ons, the rancher-monitoring add-on is disabled.

00:00:15.830 --> 00:00:18.220
Open its action menu,

00:00:18.220 --> 00:00:28.720
and select Enable. It deploys Prometheus, Grafana, Alertmanager, and the exporters that collect host and virtual machine metrics.

00:00:28.720 --> 00:00:34.590
A few minutes later, the add-on reports Deploy Successful.

00:00:34.590 --> 00:00:42.310
Let it collect for about ten minutes, the part of the recording that was fast-forwarded. Then open Dashboard.

00:00:42.310 --> 00:00:48.080
Below the capacity summary, select Cluster Metrics.

00:00:48.080 --> 00:00:57.860
The panels chart the hosts' CPU utilization, load average, and memory over the last few minutes. These panels come from Grafana, embedded in the UI.

00:00:57.860 --> 00:01:04.370
Virtual Machine Metrics puts the busiest virtual machines side by side.

00:01:04.370 --> 00:01:14.740
CPU, memory, and storage traffic for each virtual machine. demo-load runs a cycle of CPU, memory, and disk work, so its lines rise and fall.

00:01:14.740 --> 00:01:19.340
For a single virtual machine, open demo-load from Virtual Machines,

00:01:19.340 --> 00:01:24.150
and select its Virtual Machine Metrics tab.

00:01:24.150 --> 00:01:34.960
Gauges show its current CPU and memory use, and the graphs show storage traffic, latency, and operations per second. The bursts of disk writes are easy to spot.

00:01:34.960 --> 00:01:43.080
To work with the dashboards directly, open the add-on's configuration, under Add-ons, Edit Config, and select the Grafana tab.

00:01:43.080 --> 00:01:47.380
The link opens Grafana in a new window.

00:01:47.380 --> 00:01:55.210
This Grafana instance comes with the monitoring stack, with dashboards for virtual machines, hosts, Kubernetes, and the monitoring stack itself.

00:01:55.210 --> 00:01:59.120
Open Harvester VM Dashboard.

00:01:59.120 --> 00:02:03.040
It charts CPU usage for every virtual machine in the cluster,

00:02:03.040 --> 00:02:04.750
memory usage,

00:02:04.750 --> 00:02:11.720
and storage and network traffic further down. The repeating pattern of demo-load stands out among the other workloads.

00:02:11.720 --> 00:02:17.190
For the hosts, go to Dashboards and search for Node Exporter, Nodes.

00:02:17.190 --> 00:02:20.280
Open it.

00:02:20.280 --> 00:02:25.830
Node Exporter reports what the host's operating system sees, CPU usage and load,

00:02:25.830 --> 00:02:27.480
memory,

00:02:27.480 --> 00:02:31.360
and disk and network activity, for each host in the cluster.

00:02:31.360 --> 00:02:43.630
Behind every panel is a PromQL query. Prometheus, linked from the add-on's Prometheus tab, has its own query page. This query adds up the CPU each virtual machine used over the last five minutes.

00:02:43.630 --> 00:02:45.530
Select Execute,

00:02:45.530 --> 00:02:47.880
and switch to Graph.

00:02:47.880 --> 00:02:55.470
One line per virtual machine, in CPU cores. Any metric in Grafana can be explored this way.

00:02:55.470 --> 00:03:02.557
Monitoring is on, and the same metrics are available in the UI, in Grafana dashboards, and in Prometheus queries.
